--- body ---
## Context

Tracker: 0001. Filed as an open question. **The experiment has since been run
and it is a defect** -- see "Answered" below; the framing in this section is
kept as the record of what was suspected before it was measured.

0006 keeps a library-version watermark and applies deltas via `?since=`. That
tracks *item* changes. It is not obvious that it tracks a change in the
**extracted text of an unchanged item**, and that case is real: Zotero
re-extracts an attachment when its extractor improves, and the item's own
metadata does not change when it does.

zotero/zotero#6012 shows Zotero treating this as a first-class problem.
`Zotero.SDT.getProcessorVersion()` returns an identity like `'pdf/3/1'` and its
in-tree comment says it exists **"for consumers that store data derived from
packs, to fold into their staleness keys"**. Zotero also hashes each chunk's
source text (`sourceHash`) so an unchanged item is skipped and a changed one is
re-embedded regardless of why it changed. Both mechanisms exist because item
version alone is not sufficient.

zoteus stores data derived from Zotero's extraction. If the extractor changes
what a PDF yields, our index holds text that no longer matches what Zotero
would return -- silently, and until a manual rebuild.

## Why this is a question and not yet a finding

0006's ticket records that `fullTextSince` "already exists on both clients", so
there **may** already be a full-text version channel separate from item
versions, and the delta may already consult it. I have not read that path, and
`fulltext.sqlite` carries its own `fulltextIndexState(itemID, version)`
independent of `zotero.sqlite`'s item versions -- which suggests the two
sequences are genuinely separate.

So: establish the behaviour before designing anything. State the observation,
hold the cause. *(That was the right instinct, and the answer turned out to be
neither of the two outcomes anticipated below: the delta neither misses the
re-extraction nor handles it correctly. It over-reports, by comparing the right
channel against the wrong sequence.)*

## The experiment

1. Build an index over a small library including one PDF attachment.
2. Force Zotero to re-extract that attachment's full text without touching the
   item's metadata (delete `.zotero-ft-cache` and reopen in Zotero, or use
   Zotero's own re-index action).
3. Read `Last-Modified-Version` and `?since=` before and after. Does either
   surface the item?
4. Read `/items/<key>/fulltext` and check whether its own version moved.
5. Run zoteus's delta and check whether the passage text updated.

If the delta already picks it up, close this ticket with the evidence -- that is
a valuable negative result and the ticket has done its job. If it does not,
the fix follows Zotero's shape: fold an extraction identity into the staleness
key, and prefer a content hash over a version comparison, since a hash is
robust to *why* the text changed. *(Outcome: neither. See below.)*

## Answered 2026-08-22 -- and the answer is a defect, not a reassurance

**The delta does consult the right channel.** `addNewlyExtracted()` in
`delta.ts` calls `ctx.router.fullTextSince(run.watermark, ...)`, resolves each
returned attachment to its parent item, and folds it into the re-index set. Its
comment states the exact reasoning this ticket was filed to check: *"extraction
touches the attachment, and the parent item — the one the index holds passages
for — need not appear in a top-level `?since=` page at all."* So 0006's author
anticipated the hole.

**But the watermark passed to it is the wrong sequence.** Measured on the live
local API:

| | |
|---|---|
| library version (`Last-Modified-Version` on `/items`) | **410** |
| full-text versions from `/fulltext?since=0` | min 0, median **12 884**, max **25 036** |
| entries returned by `/fulltext?since=200` | **7 453 of 8 037** |

`index_meta` stores exactly one watermark (`builtFromVersion`, plus
`indexBackend` and `fulltextPending` — no full-text watermark exists).
`delta.ts:185` reads it once and uses it for both the item `?since=` pass and
`fullTextSince`. Since full-text versions run two orders of magnitude above the
library version, **`fullTextSince` reports essentially the whole library as
newly extracted on every delta.**

## What that costs

It does not run away — `maxItems` caps the re-index set at 500 items, or 50
when full text is on. The damage is subtler:

- **The genuinely re-extracted items are lost.** Candidates are taken in
  map-iteration order from a set of ~7 400, so the handful that actually
  changed are almost certainly not among the first 50.
- **Each candidate costs a request.** `addNewlyExtracted` issues a `getItem`
  per attachment to find its parent — up to `maxItems` extra requests on a
  delta whose budget this chantier states as one request when nothing changed.
  (The one-request budget itself holds: an unchanged library version returns
  `fresh` before any of this runs.)
- **It cannot converge.** The watermark advances along the library sequence, so
  the full-text comparison is no closer next time.

## The irony, recorded because it is the lesson

0006 guards *carefully* against exactly this class of error across backends —
that is what the `indexBackend` label is for, and `delta.ts:194` refuses to
compare when the label differs, saying the sequences are unrelated. It then
compares two unrelated sequences **inside** one backend. Guarding one instance
of a defect class does not guard the class.

## A note 0010 left here for whoever touched `delta.ts` next

That was this ticket. 0010 recorded that the same principle it settled --
refuse rather than launch an unrequested long rebuild behind a caller's back --
bites 0006's background rebuild path in `delta.ts`, which this fix touches.
Nothing changed there: a rebuild triggered by a *stale or unattributable
watermark* is a different case from one triggered by *corruption*. The first is
the index doing its job on a library that moved; the second is an error
condition the user has to know about. The distinction is worth keeping in mind
if that path is ever revisited.

## Actions

1. Store a **second watermark**, `fulltextVersion`, in `index_meta` beside
   `builtFromVersion`, and pass it to `fullTextSince`. It advances only when a
   full-text delta is applied.
2. Seed it correctly on a first build: the largest value seen from
   `fullTextSince(0)`, or 0 with the first delta accepted as a full sweep —
   pick one and say which, because seeding it wrong reproduces this bug in the
   other direction and hides it (nothing would ever look newly extracted).
3. Label it like the item watermark. Local and cloud full-text sequences have
   no more reason to be commensurable than the item ones do.

## Test

Red step: a fake client whose full-text versions are an order of magnitude
above its library versions — the real shape — asserting the delta requests
`fullTextSince` with the full-text watermark rather than the library one, and
that a second delta with nothing changed returns no candidates. Against today's
code the second assertion fails, because every attachment is still "newer" than
a library version.

## Exit criteria

- [x] The behaviour is established by experiment and recorded -- it over-reports
      rather than misses, and the measurement is now a committed artifact,
      `bench/results/0012-fulltext-sequence/sequences.json`, produced by
      `bench/fulltext_sequence.py` against the live local API
- [x] A separate, labelled full-text watermark, seeded deliberately
- [x] Test asserting a no-change second delta produces no candidates
      (`tests/features/search-delta-fulltext-watermark.test.ts`)

## The evidence, committed 2026-08-22

The figures below were first reported from an uncaptured call, and a review
found nothing behind them. Re-run through a committed driver that writes the
raw version list into its own output, every one reproduces:

| | |
|---|---|
| library version (`Last-Modified-Version` on `/items`) | **410** |
| full-text versions, `/fulltext?since=0` | **0 … 25 036**, median **12 884**, 8 037 entries |
| entries returned by `/fulltext?since=200` | **7 453** |
| entries returned by `/fulltext?since=410` -- the real case | **7 453**, i.e. **92,7%** |

## How it was fixed, and the two decisions inside that

**Seeded from the build's own read, not from an extra request.** The build
already calls `/fulltext?since=0` in `createFulltextSource`, to learn which
attachments have text at all; its values *are* the full-text sequence. Taking
the maximum there costs nothing and makes the FIRST delta after a build correct.
The alternative this ticket offered -- seed 0 and accept one full sweep -- would
have reproduced the defect once, deliberately.

The seed is withheld when the attachment walk did not locate every extracted
attachment (`mapped < total`). Seeding past attachments that were never indexed
is the failure this ticket warned about in the other direction, where nothing
ever looks newly extracted again.

**Swept in ascending version order, advanced only past completed versions.**
Map order plus an unmoved watermark is a loop that makes no progress -- it is
the shape the defect had. Ordering by version means a sweep cut short by the
item ceiling still leaves a version below which everything has been folded in,
so the next delta resumes there. A version group in which an attachment failed
to resolve is not counted as completed.

**No label of its own.** Local and cloud full-text sequences are no more
commensurable than the item ones, so this ticket asked for a label. It does not
need one: the backend-label check on the ITEM watermark runs before any of this,
a mismatch rebuilds, and a rebuild clears both watermarks together. They are
written together and cleared together, so no path can leave one attributable and
the other not. Pinned by the test rather than argued.
